WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.316
Welcome to Module 2 of the
Non-Person Identity learning path:

2
00:00:04.316 --> 00:00:06.875
owners, sponsors and the orphan rule.

3
00:00:06.875 --> 00:00:11.675
In Module 1 you read every non-person
identity into one register.

4
00:00:11.675 --> 00:00:13.875
Now you check who answers for each one.

5
00:00:13.875 --> 00:00:18.888
Ownership is part of Gate 1, Inventory,
and the orphan rule is part of Gate 6,

6
00:00:18.888 --> 00:00:23.400
Lifecycle, and both come from
Chapter 2 of the developer workbook.

7
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.993
You'll check every identity for two human
owners and an active service record,

8
00:00:27.993 --> 00:00:30.935
and build the orphan rule
as a plan that a reviewer

9
00:00:30.935 --> 00:00:33.662
approves by its hash
before anything changes.

10
00:00:33.662 --> 00:00:37.601
The code runs on your laptop,
against the same fictional estate.

11
00:00:37.601 --> 00:00:42.970
It's illustrative: tested against
fixtures, not run against a live tenant.

12
00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:48.770
Why does ownership matter so much?

13
00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:52.436
Because, as the program's
guidance on app ownership warns,

14
00:00:52.436 --> 00:00:56.257
an owner can add a credential to
an app, and then act as the app.

15
00:00:56.257 --> 00:00:59.446
So owners are a control,
not a contact list.

16
00:00:59.446 --> 00:01:04.582
The program wants two human owners on every
app registration and service principal,

17
00:01:04.582 --> 00:01:08.324
a record in the system inventory,
and an application owner for

18
00:01:08.324 --> 00:01:12.507
every Active Directory account,
found before the account is touched.

19
00:01:12.507 --> 00:01:15.235
An identity with no owner is an orphan,

20
00:01:15.235 --> 00:01:18.932
and the plan's decision D N
P 2 says what happens to it.

21
00:01:18.932 --> 00:01:21.920
Whatever happens, the
rule is never delete.

22
00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:26.458
In this module you check ownership as
code, and you build the orphan rule

23
00:01:26.458 --> 00:01:31.270
as a plan that a reviewer approves by
its hash, before anything changes.

24
00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:37.858
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

25
00:01:37.858 --> 00:01:40.245
which takes about sixty
minutes with the lab.

26
00:01:40.245 --> 00:01:42.620
You'll state the program's ownership rule,

27
00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:44.995
and which owners the code counts as valid.

28
00:01:44.995 --> 00:01:47.484
You'll explain D N P 2, the orphan rule,

29
00:01:47.484 --> 00:01:52.120
and how it differs from the program's
own policy for orphaned apps.

30
00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:56.258
You'll run the ownership check and
read its findings and its orphan plan.

31
00:01:56.258 --> 00:02:00.598
And you'll show that the write path
only dry-runs without the Apply switch,

32
00:02:00.598 --> 00:02:03.346
and refuses a plan whose
hash nobody approved.

33
00:02:03.346 --> 00:02:08.970
Before you start, finish Module 1, because
this module checks Module 1's register.

34
00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:12.264
And read the guide's record
each identity joins to,

35
00:02:12.264 --> 00:02:14.433
for the owner and sponsor fields.

36
00:02:14.433 --> 00:02:20.532
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7.1 or later with
Pester 5, and a clone of the repository.

37
00:02:20.532 --> 00:02:21.810
No tenant.

38
00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.048
Unit 1 is the guide.

39
00:02:27.048 --> 00:02:32.098
Step 1 f joins each identity to its
record and names who answers for it.

40
00:02:32.098 --> 00:02:35.746
In the program's design guidance,
every app registration and

41
00:02:35.746 --> 00:02:39.686
service principal gets two human
owners, never service accounts.

42
00:02:39.686 --> 00:02:43.039
The Owners list holds only the
people who configure the app;

43
00:02:43.039 --> 00:02:47.830
the business approver goes in the registry
and in the service management reference,

44
00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:50.910
because an owner can add a
credential and act as the app.

45
00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:55.623
And each identity gets a sponsor who
is a federal employee mastered in HR.

46
00:02:55.623 --> 00:03:00.261
A contractor can build or run a
workload, but can't be its only sponsor.

47
00:03:00.261 --> 00:03:02.610
Then Step 6 runs the lifecycle.

48
00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:05.266
An owner who leaves or
changes roles starts

49
00:03:05.266 --> 00:03:07.922
a transfer at least ten
business days ahead.

50
00:03:07.922 --> 00:03:10.473
An app with no named owner is an orphan.

51
00:03:10.473 --> 00:03:15.191
And decommissioning always disables
first, then deletes; Microsoft Learn's

52
00:03:15.191 --> 00:03:20.650
Deactivate an app registration documentation
describes the reversible switch.

53
00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:26.987
Unit 2 reads the rule the code holds.

54
00:03:26.987 --> 00:03:32.223
Microsoft Learn's enterprise application
ownership documentation recommends at least

55
00:03:32.223 --> 00:03:36.620
two owners where possible, and its Get
M G Service Principal Owner reference

56
00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:40.738
describes a service principal's owners
as users or service principals.

57
00:03:40.738 --> 00:03:44.224
The program's rule is stricter,
and it's design guidance.

58
00:03:44.224 --> 00:03:49.625
Two human owners, never service accounts,
and not service principals or guests:

59
00:03:49.625 --> 00:03:53.862
the code counts an owner only if
it's a user, a member, and enabled.

60
00:03:53.862 --> 00:03:56.581
A service management
reference that names a

61
00:03:56.581 --> 00:03:59.300
record in the system
inventory, not free text.

62
00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:02.944
And for every Active Directory
account, an application owner,

63
00:04:02.944 --> 00:04:06.017
which comes from an owners
file kept with the record,

64
00:04:06.017 --> 00:04:09.875
because the directory has no owners
attribute the code can trust.

65
00:04:09.875 --> 00:04:11.850
Then disable, never delete.

66
00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:17.093
Microsoft Learn's Deactivate an app
registration procedure is on the beta endpoint,

67
00:04:17.093 --> 00:04:20.839
so the code disables the tenant's
service principal instead,

68
00:04:20.839 --> 00:04:22.630
on version one point oh.

69
00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:28.080
Now the orphan rule itself.

70
00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.626
The plan's decision D N P 2,
which the C I S O owns, says:

71
00:04:31.626 --> 00:04:37.655
disable the identity once the identity
governance officer confirms the finding,

72
00:04:37.655 --> 00:04:42.443
then allow thirty days for the office
to name an owner or retire it.

73
00:04:42.443 --> 00:04:43.455
Never delete.

74
00:04:43.455 --> 00:04:48.454
The program's own governance policy for
orphaned apps runs the other way round:

75
00:04:48.454 --> 00:04:53.068
thirty days to assign an owner, then
access is suspended on day thirty-one.

76
00:04:53.068 --> 00:04:58.243
And the program's rule for AI agents
disables first, because an agent can act.

77
00:04:58.243 --> 00:05:02.266
The program settled on D N P
2, the stricter of the two:

78
00:05:02.266 --> 00:05:05.618
disable first, then
thirty days to claim it.

79
00:05:05.618 --> 00:05:08.643
The guide's Orphans step
and the code both follow it.

80
00:05:08.643 --> 00:05:13.468
Microsoft Learn's Governing Microsoft
Entra service accounts documentation

81
00:05:13.468 --> 00:05:18.210
agrees on the shape: disable, but don't
delete until the review is complete.

82
00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:27.554
The check reads each owner once,
with only User dot Read dot All,

83
00:05:27.554 --> 00:05:31.722
and applies nine rules, N P
I O W N oh one to oh nine.

84
00:05:31.722 --> 00:05:35.359
Oh one is an app registration
or legacy service principal

85
00:05:35.359 --> 00:05:39.147
with no valid human owner, which
makes it an orphan candidate.

86
00:05:39.147 --> 00:05:41.760
Oh two is one valid owner, not two.

87
00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:44.423
Oh three, four and five are warnings:

88
00:05:44.423 --> 00:05:49.473
an owner that's a service principal,
a guest, or disabled or not found.

89
00:05:49.473 --> 00:05:52.246
Oh six is no service management reference,

90
00:05:52.246 --> 00:05:56.209
and oh seven a reference that
names no record, or a retired one.

91
00:05:56.209 --> 00:05:59.345
Oh eight is an Active
Directory account with no valid

92
00:05:59.345 --> 00:06:03.122
application owner in the owners
file, another orphan candidate.

93
00:06:03.122 --> 00:06:05.307
And oh nine, information only,

94
00:06:05.307 --> 00:06:10.222
is an account Module 1 found only
by a password that never expires.

95
00:06:10.222 --> 00:06:15.800
The fields for AI sponsors are
recorded, empty, for Module 9 to check.

96
00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:23.585
The check's orphan candidates and
the owners' claims become a plan,

97
00:06:23.585 --> 00:06:26.425
one action each, and
nothing in the plan writes.

98
00:06:26.425 --> 00:06:30.488
Only two kinds of action can
write, after review: Disable,

99
00:06:30.488 --> 00:06:34.375
and AddOwner, for a claim
that names an enabled member.

100
00:06:34.375 --> 00:06:38.532
The rest only record: waiting
for the officer's confirmation,

101
00:06:38.532 --> 00:06:43.187
a claim window still open, a retirement
candidate, a refused nominee.

102
00:06:43.187 --> 00:06:48.663
The same inputs give the same plan, byte
for byte, so a reviewer approves a hash.

103
00:06:48.663 --> 00:06:53.713
Then a separate script, the only code in
the example that can write, runs the plan.

104
00:06:53.713 --> 00:06:58.450
It refuses unless the plan's hash is
the approved one, the plan is for this

105
00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:03.349
tenant and cloud and no more than seven
days old, and each write is declared.

106
00:07:03.349 --> 00:07:07.111
The only PATCH body it allows
sets account enabled to false,

107
00:07:07.111 --> 00:07:08.800
and it never sends a delete.

108
00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:11.644
And a live run is a person's
session, never an app's,

109
00:07:11.644 --> 00:07:16.383
because Microsoft Learn's Graph
permissions reference warns that this write

110
00:07:16.383 --> 00:07:19.227
permission can let an app
act as other entities,

111
00:07:19.227 --> 00:07:23.160
and the program's rule is that
disabling is never automatic.

112
00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:27.635
Try it.

113
00:07:27.635 --> 00:07:30.961
Lab 1 runs in the
non-person identity folder,

114
00:07:30.961 --> 00:07:34.010
in PowerShell 7, on the fictional estate.

115
00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:38.185
Step one makes Module 1's register,
because this check reads it.

116
00:07:38.185 --> 00:07:42.904
Step two sends the samples from
Microsoft Learn's Graph applications

117
00:07:42.904 --> 00:07:47.298
tutorial to the fake: ten pass, and
the twenty not run are Chapter 1's.

118
00:07:47.298 --> 00:07:49.948
Step three lists what each task needs.

119
00:07:49.948 --> 00:07:55.509
The ownership check needs only User dot Read
dot All: it reads people, nothing else.

120
00:07:55.509 --> 00:07:57.084
The write task is separate,

121
00:07:57.084 --> 00:08:00.660
and it's the one that holds
Application dot ReadWrite dot All.

122
00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:03.522
Step four checks ownership
and builds the plan.

123
00:08:03.522 --> 00:08:07.773
Twenty-two identities are checked,
and seven are orphan candidates.

124
00:08:07.773 --> 00:08:11.861
That's twenty findings, three
warnings and one information item.

125
00:08:11.861 --> 00:08:13.968
The orphan plan has ten actions,

126
00:08:13.968 --> 00:08:17.535
and three of them write: one
AddOwner and two disables.

127
00:08:17.535 --> 00:08:18.710
Nothing is sent.

128
00:08:18.710 --> 00:08:23.030
And the plan's hash is the same
on every run with these inputs.

129
00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:29.218
Step five groups the findings by rule.

130
00:08:29.218 --> 00:08:34.754
Two apps have no valid owner at all:
Legacy Reports and Old SAML Connector.

131
00:08:34.754 --> 00:08:36.680
Four have only one valid owner.

132
00:08:36.680 --> 00:08:40.075
Build Pipeline's second
owner is a service principal,

133
00:08:40.075 --> 00:08:43.393
and Vendor Portal's is a guest,
so each counts only one.

134
00:08:43.393 --> 00:08:47.706
Six have no service management reference,
three have a reference to a record

135
00:08:47.706 --> 00:08:52.155
that isn't active, and five directory
accounts have no valid application owner.

136
00:08:52.155 --> 00:08:54.755
Step six reads the plan's ten actions.

137
00:08:54.755 --> 00:08:58.984
Three write: Disable for svc
backup and for Legacy Reports,

138
00:08:58.984 --> 00:09:02.067
and AddOwner for HR
Connector, from a claim.

139
00:09:02.067 --> 00:09:04.728
svc reports is a retirement candidate:

140
00:09:04.728 --> 00:09:08.304
it was disabled, and nobody
claimed it in thirty days.

141
00:09:08.304 --> 00:09:13.230
And the claim for Build Pipeline named
a guest, so the nominee is refused.

142
00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:21.005
Step seven computes the plan's hash
and dry-runs the plan with it.

143
00:09:21.005 --> 00:09:25.470
Without the Apply switch, the write
path runs its prechecks and stops,

144
00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:30.169
reporting three writes it would send:
a directory disable for svc backup,

145
00:09:30.169 --> 00:09:34.085
a PATCH that turns off the Legacy
Reports service principal,

146
00:09:34.085 --> 00:09:36.905
and a POST that adds an
owner to HR Connector.

147
00:09:36.905 --> 00:09:40.655
Step eight sends them to the
fake with Apply: three sent.

148
00:09:40.655 --> 00:09:45.031
In the results file, each disable
tells the operator to record disabled

149
00:09:45.031 --> 00:09:49.630
on October thirtieth, and that the
claim window ends November twenty-ninth.

150
00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:52.819
Then you run the same plan
with a hash of all zeros,

151
00:09:52.819 --> 00:09:57.205
which nobody approved, and it refuses
to run a plan nobody reviewed.

152
00:09:57.205 --> 00:10:01.317
Step nine runs every test:
five hundred thirteen pass.

153
00:10:01.317 --> 00:10:06.170
Delete the output folder when you're done,
because it names accounts and owners.

154
00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:14.163
Lab 2 runs the rule in your own
test tenant: worldwide or G C C,

155
00:10:14.163 --> 00:10:18.140
with a test Active Directory
domain, never production,

156
00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:22.383
with fictional people, and with
Module 1's Lab 2 already run.

157
00:10:22.383 --> 00:10:26.299
Make two test orphans: an app
registration with no owners,

158
00:10:26.299 --> 00:10:28.132
and a test service account.

159
00:10:28.132 --> 00:10:31.857
Run the check with an app that
holds only User dot Read dot All.

160
00:10:31.857 --> 00:10:35.533
Confirm both orphans as the
identity governance officer.

161
00:10:35.533 --> 00:10:38.266
Then have a second person
approve the plan's hash,

162
00:10:38.266 --> 00:10:41.408
which is the separation of
duties the evidence records.

163
00:10:41.408 --> 00:10:43.249
Dry-run the plan, then apply it,

164
00:10:43.249 --> 00:10:47.083
signed in as a person with
Cloud Application Administrator.

165
00:10:47.083 --> 00:10:50.806
To back it out, step six turns
the service principal back on,

166
00:10:50.806 --> 00:10:54.141
enables the test account,
deletes both test objects

167
00:10:54.141 --> 00:10:57.632
and the test app's registration,
and deletes the output.

168
00:10:57.632 --> 00:11:00.140
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

169
00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:05.277
The federal requirement.

170
00:11:05.277 --> 00:11:09.813
Ownership is part of Gate 1 and
the orphan rule part of Gate 6,

171
00:11:09.813 --> 00:11:12.960
and the guide's validate
steps name A C two,

172
00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:17.403
A C two enhancement three, I A
four and C M eight, among others.

173
00:11:17.403 --> 00:11:20.639
The module's table maps
each one to its evidence.

174
00:11:20.639 --> 00:11:24.653
For account management, the ownership
file and the owner findings.

175
00:11:24.653 --> 00:11:28.045
For identifier management
and the component inventory,

176
00:11:28.045 --> 00:11:31.365
the findings that each identity
names an active record.

177
00:11:31.365 --> 00:11:35.865
For disabling accounts and change
control, the orphan plan with its hash,

178
00:11:35.865 --> 00:11:38.565
and the results and
manifest from the apply.

179
00:11:38.565 --> 00:11:42.029
For separation of duties,
the officer's confirmations,

180
00:11:42.029 --> 00:11:44.877
and the plan hash approved
by a second person.

181
00:11:44.877 --> 00:11:48.553
And for least privilege, the
permission lists for the two tasks.

182
00:11:48.553 --> 00:11:52.997
K S I's are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers;

183
00:11:52.997 --> 00:11:58.280
the agency lines its evidence up with
them, and the mapping is the program's.

184
00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:04.005
Check yourself with two questions.

185
00:12:04.005 --> 00:12:07.130
First: which owners does
the code count as valid?

186
00:12:07.130 --> 00:12:10.768
The answer is b: users who
are members and enabled.

187
00:12:10.768 --> 00:12:14.682
A guest or a service principal
listed as an owner raises a warning,

188
00:12:14.682 --> 00:12:16.567
and doesn't count toward the two.

189
00:12:16.567 --> 00:12:19.376
Second: you run the
orphan action script with

190
00:12:19.376 --> 00:12:21.956
an approved hash that
isn't the plan's hash.

191
00:12:21.956 --> 00:12:22.967
What happens?

192
00:12:22.967 --> 00:12:24.193
The answer is b.

193
00:12:24.193 --> 00:12:27.092
It refuses to run a plan nobody reviewed.

194
00:12:27.092 --> 00:12:29.856
You saw that refusal in
step eight of the lab.

195
00:12:29.856 --> 00:12:32.237
The module page has three more questions,

196
00:12:32.237 --> 00:12:36.020
each linked to the section that
answers it, including how the code

197
00:12:36.020 --> 00:12:40.300
disables an orphaned app, and why
the write path is a person's session.

198
00:12:44.050 --> 00:12:44.875
To sum up.

199
00:12:44.875 --> 00:12:49.253
You checked twenty-two identities
for valid owners and active records,

200
00:12:49.253 --> 00:12:53.111
built an orphan plan of ten actions
with three writes, dry-ran it,

201
00:12:53.111 --> 00:12:57.637
applied it to the fake, and saw the
write path refuse a hash nobody approved.

202
00:12:57.637 --> 00:13:02.886
The evidence an ISSO keeps from this module
is the ownership file, the findings,

203
00:13:02.886 --> 00:13:07.650
and the orphan plan with its approved
hash; the officer's confirmations;

204
00:13:07.650 --> 00:13:12.737
and the dry-run and apply folders, with
their results and manifests, all dated.

205
00:13:12.737 --> 00:13:15.813
Before you ship, work through
the chapter's checklist.

206
00:13:15.813 --> 00:13:19.774
Next is Module 3: decide
each identity's target.

207
00:13:19.774 --> 00:13:23.426
A matrix proposes where each
identity should end up,

208
00:13:23.426 --> 00:13:27.660
the application owner decides,
and the ISSO checks the gate.
